import VerifiedGarbage.TCB.Mem

/-!
# SEED and ECB

**Trusted.** SEED is transcribed from RFC 4269 (December 2005):
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4269.txt. The Feistel structure is §2,
the round function F §2.1, the function G §2.2 (its original form, from the
two S-boxes and the masks `m0`–`m3`, not the extended SS-boxes), the key
schedule and its constants `KCi` §2.3, decryption §2.4 (the encryption
procedure with the round keys in reverse order), and the S-boxes S0 and S1
Appendix A.1, in printed order. ECB applies the cipher independently to
each block (SP 800-38A §6.1).

SEED has 128-bit blocks and 128-bit keys. RFC 4269 numbers bits and words
most significant first: the 128-bit input `L || R`, `L = L0 || L1` and
`R = R0 || R1`, and the key `Key0 || Key1 || Key2 || Key3` are read from
bytes in big-endian order (Appendix B's intermediate values show it), and
`X = X3 || X2 || X1 || X0` in G makes `X0` the least significant byte.

All key bytes, round keys and data are secret in the contracts; S-box
indexing here does not permit secret-dependent memory access in an
implementation.
-/

namespace VG.Spec.Seed

abbrev Block := Vector Byte 16
abbrev Word := BitVec 32
/-- The round keys, `K1,0`, `K1,1`, `K2,0`, …, `K16,1`: round `i`'s
`Ki0` (the most significant half of `Ki`) and `Ki1`, at `2 * (i - 1)` and
`2 * (i - 1) + 1`. -/
abbrev Schedule := Vector Word 32

/-- Appendix A.1, S-box S0. -/
